course: dictionary
lesson: 1328
title: Calluses: hard skin and healing bones
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A callus is a patch of hard, thick skin. Your skin makes one when something rubs or presses on the same spot again and again. The sentence «after a few weeks of short regular practice, the skin toughens into calluses, patches of hardened skin, and the pain fades» gives you clues: toughens and hardened tell you the skin has become harder.
explain: Context helps with the science meaning too. «Bone heals in stages, a blood clot, then soft new bone called a callus that slowly hardens.» Soft new bone that slowly hardens is growing over a break, so a callus here is the hard lump that forms around a broken bone as it heals.
explain: Always check by swapping in your meaning. In «Rubbing makes skin grow thicker into calluses», try "patches of hard skin" in place of calluses. It still makes sense, so your meaning is right.
fact: A callus is a patch of hard, thick skin made by rubbing or pressing again and again.
fact: The plural of callus is calluses: one callus, many calluses.
fact: Guitar players often get calluses on their fingertips after a few weeks of practice.
fact: In science, a callus is the new bone that forms around a broken bone and slowly hardens as it heals.
fact: Healing a broken bone can take several weeks, even in children.
exercise: «Guitar strings hurt beginners' fingertips.» What is a callus? | A string on a guitar | A sore blister full of water | A bone that has broken | *A patch of hard, thick skin
exercise: «Bone heals in stages... soft new bone called a callus that slowly hardens.» In science, what is a callus? | A kind of shoe worn by runners | A soft lump that never changes | A blood clot | *The hard lump that forms around a broken bone as it heals
exercise: «Barefoot runners grow thick soles that protect like shoes.» What do the thick soles do? | Turn into bone | Make running slower | Make the feet shine | *Keep the feet safe, like shoes
test: Why does skin grow calluses? | Because the skin is wet | Because it is cold outside | Because a bone has broken | *Because rubbing or pressing happens again and again
test: In «a blister cushions a sore spot», what does cushions mean? | Cuts open | *Protects softly | Hurts badly | Hides away
exam: Which is the best way to check the meaning of a new word from context? | Guess quickly and move on | Skip the sentence and ignore it | *Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense | Look only at the first letter
